// ---------------- The curriculum, part five: the deep pass ----------------
// Lessons rebuilt from published course outlines (NFTS MFA outlines, the AFI Conservatory catalogue, Berklee screen scoring,
// the DGA assistant director trainee programme). Appended after the earlier lessons so existing lesson ids keep their numbers.
const CURR_DEEP = {
  dir: [
    [1, 'Directing from a script you didn\'t write', 'Most working directors interpret other people\'s pages. The craft is finding the heart of a scene in someone else\'s words, then finding the visual language to bring it to the screen clearly.', 'Read the scene three times: once for story, once for each character\'s want, once for what is never said. Write one sentence for "what this scene is really about" before you plan a single shot.', ['The set text is a dull breakfast scene.', ['Find the subtext: what is nobody saying?', 'vstory', 'The eggs go cold while a marriage ends in silence.'], ['Liven it up with camera moves', 'stag', 'It looks busy and says nothing new.']], 'Tutors still screen the breakfast scene in {F} ({Y}): {D} shot a page of small talk as a quiet war.'],
    [1, 'Learn to act so you can direct actors', 'Directing courses put directors on the floor as actors, using their own body and voice, so they learn how an actor prepares, rehearses and protects themselves on set.', 'Prepare a short scene as an actor: objectives, obstacles, the "as if". Then swap and direct a classmate, noticing which of your notes actually helped you when you were acting.', ['A classmate gives you a result note: "be sadder".', ['Translate it into an action you can play', 'dact', 'You discover why playable notes work.'], ['Try to look sadder', 'range', 'It reads as acting, and you know it.']], ''],
    [1, 'The no-dialogue exercise', 'A classic school exercise: a short film with almost no words, limited actors, one location and a few rolls of film. Limits force you to tell story through staging, image and sound.', 'Write a story that can be understood with the sound off. Plan it in images: who enters, what they see, what changes. Count your words, and cut any that explain what the picture already shows.', ['Your story only makes sense with a line of explanation.', ['Rethink the action so the line isn\'t needed', 'vstory', 'The audience works it out and enjoys working it out.'], ['Keep the line', 'dial', 'It works, but the exercise was to do without.']], '{D} cut every line from the opening ten minutes of {F} ({Y}); nobody noticed they were missing.'],
    [2, 'The director\'s book', 'Before each shoot a director builds a book: script analysis, each scene\'s purpose and beats, character arcs, references, shot lists, floor plans and blocking diagrams. Tutors critique the book, not just the film.', 'For every scene: the event (what changes), each character\'s objective, the beat breakdown, a shot list motivated by those beats, and an overhead plan showing actors and camera.', ['Your shot list has 40 setups for a two-day shoot.', ['Cut to the setups each beat really needs', 'setm', 'You make your day and every shot means something.'], ['Keep them all and shoot fast', 'speed', 'You lose the last scene to the clock.']], ''],
    [2, 'Blocking: no decision should be arbitrary', 'Camera and actor movement should come from the text. Directors are asked to justify every shot, move and edit: why here, why this size, why does she cross the room now?', 'Mark where the power shifts in the scene. Stage a move on each shift, and let the camera respond. If you cannot say why a shot exists, cut it.', ['An actor asks why they cross to the window.', ['Give a reason from the character\'s need', 'dact', 'The move becomes behaviour, not traffic.'], ['"It looks good for the camera"', 'stag', 'Honest, but the actor plays it empty.']], 'The single-take argument in {F} ({Y}) is studied because every step {A} takes changes who is winning.'],
    [2, 'Genre and comedy as craft', 'Genre is a contract with the audience. Horror, comedy and thrillers each have techniques (timing, withholding, the reveal) that a director must control, especially directors who usually work outside them.', 'Pick a genre you never watch. Break down three scenes from it: setup, delay, payoff. Then shoot a short homework scene in that genre, playing it straight.', ['Your comedy scene isn\'t funny in the edit.', ['Recut for timing: hold, then cut on the laugh', 'comic', 'The pause was the joke.'], ['Add music to tell people it\'s funny', 'score', 'Now it\'s a scene about music.']], '', 'Comedy'],
    [3, 'Improvisation as a directing tool', 'Improv is not just for comedy. Directors use it in rehearsal to find behaviour, loosen dialogue, test a scene\'s logic and improve communication with actors and crew.', 'Rehearse the situation, not the lines: give the actors the circumstances and objectives and let them play it in their own words. Steal what is alive and put it back into the script.', ['A scene feels stiff in rehearsal.', ['Improvise the moment before the scene', 'impro', 'The actors arrive already in the middle of something.'], ['Drill the lines until they\'re smooth', 'stag', 'Smooth and lifeless.']], 'The best line in {F} ({Y}) came from a rehearsal improv; {D} wrote it into the script that night.'],
    [3, 'Preparing your first feature', 'A first feature needs a package: the script, a look book, a director\'s statement, a sense of budget, cast ideas, and a pitch that makes people trust you with millions. Schools also have directors write a five-year career plan.', 'Write a one-page director\'s statement (why you, why now), a look book with references, and a two-minute pitch. Rehearse it for agents, producers and executives, who all listen for different things.', ['An executive asks, "Why are you the one to make this?"', ['Tell the personal connection, briefly', 'pres', 'They lean in.'], ['List your film-school credits', 'pack', 'They nod and check the time.']], ''],
  ],
  wri: [
    [1, 'Finding the stories only you can tell', 'Screenwriting courses begin inward: who are you as a storyteller and what do you want to say? Your voice is your currency, and the exercises mine your own life, experiences and passions.', 'Write ten things you believe that most people don\'t. Write five moments you\'ve never told anyone about. Develop three short-film treatments from them; one gets made.', ['Your three ideas are all genre exercises.', ['Develop the one that scares you to write', 'theme', 'It\'s the one producers remember.'], ['Pick the most commercial', 'mkt', 'Competent, and indistinguishable from the pile.']], ''],
    [1, 'The ten-minute two-hander', 'Writers learn a lot from the stage: a ten-minute play for two actors, rehearsed with a theatre director and performed for an audience. It teaches dramatising through dialogue and action in real time, with nowhere to cut.', 'Two people, one place, one thing at stake that changes hands. Hear it read aloud by actors, then rewrite: theatre exposes every line that only gives information.', ['At the read, the actors stumble over a speech.', ['Cut it to what the character would really say', 'dial', 'It plays in half the time, twice as hard.'], ['Ask the actors to rehearse it more', 'struc', 'The speech is still the problem.']], ''],
    [1, 'Treatment, step outline, draft', 'Short films and pilots move through stages: an expanded treatment, a scene-by-scene outline, then first and second drafts, each one locked before the next stage. Scripts are locked before production begins.', 'Write the treatment in present tense prose. Turn it into a numbered list of scenes with one line each for what happens and what changes. Only draft once every scene earns its place.', ['Your outline has a saggy middle.', ['Fix it in the outline before drafting', 'struc', 'Two hours of work instead of two weeks.'], ['Start the draft and find it as you go', 'speed', 'You find it eventually, three drafts later.']], ''],
    [2, 'Writing script reports and taking notes', 'Producers and writers learn to give and take notes: what works, what doesn\'t, and why. A good note names the problem and leaves the solution to the writer.', 'When reading: summarise the story in a paragraph, note where you lost interest and why, and separate taste from craft. When receiving notes: look for "the note behind the note".', ['A producer says, "Can the hero be more likeable?"', ['Ask what scene lost them', 'cha', 'The real problem is one cruel moment with no reason.'], ['Add a scene of the hero saving a dog', 'char', 'It\'s the cliché it sounds like.']], ''],
    [2, 'The writers\' room', 'Television writing runs through the room. Writers break each other\'s stories, pitch alternatives, then go away and write their own episode while honouring the show\'s voice.', 'Break a story on the board: the A, B and C plots, act breaks, the button at the end of each act. In the room, pitch solutions, not complaints, and let the best idea win, even when it\'s not yours.', ['Your pitch is shot down in the room.', ['Build on the idea that won', 'cha', 'The showrunner notices you make things better.'], ['Defend your pitch', 'struc', 'You\'re right, and nobody cares.']], '{W} came up through the room on {F} ({Y}), known as the writer who fixed other people\'s act breaks.'],
    [2, 'Adapting intellectual property', 'Much of television is adapted from books, true stories and other IP. The task is to find the story you want to tell inside the source and build it into a series that honours the original without just copying it.', 'Read the source twice: once for pleasure, once for structure. Ask what the series is about that the book isn\'t. Note the legal and ethical questions when adapting real people\'s lives.', ['The book\'s best chapter doesn\'t fit your series.', ['Cut it and keep its spirit elsewhere', 'adapt', 'The series works as its own thing.'], ['Keep it for the fans', 'theme', 'One episode feels like a different show.']], ''],
    [3, 'Table reads with professional actors', 'Hearing your pages performed changes everything. Writing programmes cast professional actors to read the first act of a feature, then ask the writer for a revision plan based on what they heard.', 'Listen for where the room goes quiet and where it shuffles. Note lines actors paraphrased: they\'re telling you how it should be said. Write the revision plan the next morning.', ['The actors get laughs you didn\'t intend.', ['Find out why, and decide whether to keep them', 'tone', 'You learn what your script really is.'], ['Rewrite the lines to stop the laughs', 'dial', 'You lose the life along with the laughs.']], ''],
    [3, 'Bio and taster pages', 'When writers meet agents, producers and commissioners, they send a short biography and a script sample of about 20 pages. The sample is chosen to show voice, not plot.', 'Choose the 20 pages that sound most like you and most unlike anyone else. Write a bio that tells one story about why you write, not a list of jobs.', ['Your best pages are from the middle of a script.', ['Send them with a two-line setup', 'pack', 'The agent reads all 20 pages.'], ['Send the first 20 pages', 'struc', 'Fine, safe, forgettable.']], ''],
  ],
  tv: [
    [2, 'The pilot is two things at once', 'A pilot must work as a standalone story and as the opening of a long arc. Directing and writing courses analyse existing pilots for both functions.', 'List what the pilot promises: the engine that will generate stories for years, the characters we\'ll follow, and the question that the season will answer.', ['Your pilot ends with everything resolved.', ['Leave the engine running', 'struc', 'The audience wants episode two.'], ['Keep it satisfying', 'theme', 'A good short film, a bad pilot.']], ''],
    [2, 'The series bible', 'A bible sets out the world, the characters and their arcs, the tone, and episode ideas for a season or more. It\'s what buyers, directors and future writers use to keep the show consistent.', 'Write: the logline, the world\'s rules, a page on each main character (want, wound, secret), the season arc and ten episode springboards.', ['Your bible runs to 60 pages.', ['Cut to what helps someone write an episode', 'pack', 'Commissioners actually read it.'], ['Keep it all; it shows the depth', 'world', 'It shows depth to no one, because no one reads it.']], ''],
    [3, 'Running the show week to week', 'Showrunners run tone meetings, production meetings and table reads every episode while the next scripts are written and the last episodes are cut. Every department needs answers, every day.', 'Hold a tone meeting with each director: what the episode is about, what must never happen, which scenes matter most. Make decisions fast and write them down.', ['Two departments need opposite answers by noon.', ['Decide now and explain why', 'tas', 'People can work with a no.'], ['Push the decision to tomorrow', 'speed', 'Both departments lose a day.']], '{C} gave {W} a show to run after {F} ({Y}); the crew still talk about how quickly decisions came back.'],
    [3, 'Pitching to commissioners', 'Television is sold to channels and streamers whose commissioning tastes and budgets ("tariffs") differ. Producers research each buyer before pitching.', 'Before the meeting, watch the buyer\'s last three commissions and learn their slots and budget levels. Pitch the show as if it already exists on their service.', ['A buyer says your show is "not for us".', ['Ask what they are looking for', 'mkt', 'You learn their next brief before anyone else.'], ['Argue that it is', 'pres', 'A no becomes a firm no.']], ''],
  ],
  cam: [
    [1, 'Exposure, movement, framing on 16mm', 'Cinematography courses still start on film: with no monitor to trust, you learn exposure with a light meter, framing through the eyepiece and movement by rehearsal.', 'Meter the key and the shadows, decide where you want each to sit on the stock, then shoot a test roll at a range of stops to see for yourself.', ['You aren\'t sure about the exposure.', ['Shoot a test and look at it', 'light', 'You learn exactly how far the stock stretches.'], ['Expose for safety in the middle', 'col', 'It looks fine and a bit flat.']], ''],
    [1, 'Recreating a painting', 'In a classic cross-department workshop, designers build a set from a painting, cinematographers recreate its light, and VFX extends the set. You learn to read where the light in a picture comes from.', 'Study the painting: where is the source, how soft is it, what colour are the shadows? Light with one key source first, then add only what the painting shows.', ['The painting has impossible light.', ['Cheat it with a hidden second source', 'light', 'It looks exactly like the painting.'], ['Light it realistically', 'colour', 'Correct, and not the painting.']], ''],
    [1, 'The DP and the gaffer', 'Learning to lead a camera and lighting crew is part of the course. The cinematographer gives the gaffer the intention ("late afternoon, she\'s alone, warm but fading"), and the gaffer works out the units.', 'Describe the light in terms of story and time, not lamps. Let the gaffer propose the rig. Check it through the lens, and adjust with words, not by grabbing equipment.', ['The gaffer\'s rig isn\'t what you pictured.', ['Talk through the difference in story terms', 'cha', 'You get it, and keep a good gaffer.'], ['Move the lights yourself', 'light', 'Faster today. The crew stop offering ideas.']], ''],
    [2, 'Presenting the visual plan', 'A week before each shoot, cinematographers present their plan: how they read the script, and how camera, lens and lighting choices support the story. Problems are solved in that room, not on set.', 'Present: three reference images, the camera grammar (static or moving, lens range), the colour palette, the hardest scene and how you\'ll solve it, and the kit you need.', ['The producer says your plan is over budget.', ['Show which scene the money serves, and cut elsewhere', 'bud', 'You keep the shot that matters.'], ['Ask for more money', 'pres', 'You get the money and lose the producer\'s trust.']], ''],
    [2, 'Lens tests and dailies', 'Professional camera prep includes lens tests (sharpness, flare, breathing, colour) and screening dailies critically. Cinematographers learn to judge images on a big screen, not a monitor.', 'Shoot a chart and a face with every lens at the same stop. Screen them together. In dailies, note anything you\'d change tomorrow, not what went well.', ['Two lenses in the set don\'t match.', ['Swap one before the shoot', 'eye', 'The film looks like one film.'], ['Fix it in the grade', 'col', 'The colourist can fix colour, not character.']], ''],
    [2, 'Less is more: the small digital shoot', 'Small digital films are where schools push risk: a smaller crew, a small package, fewer days. You\'re assessed on how you use locations, time of day and scheduling to make the most of limited kit.', 'Scout for natural light. Schedule scenes for the hour their light is best. Let the sun be your biggest lamp, and bounce instead of adding.', ['You have one light for a night exterior.', ['Use the streetlights and expose for them', 'light', 'It looks like a real night.'], ['Light the actors flat to see them', 'col', 'You see them, and nothing else.']], ''],
    [3, 'Miniature sets and stop-motion light', 'Lighting stop-motion is precision work: tiny sets, small lamps, light that must stay identical over weeks of frame-by-frame shooting, and software like frame-capture tools that check every frame.', 'Lock everything down. Use small, controllable sources and flag them tightly. Check for flicker between frames, and block out daylight entirely.', ['Frames flicker in the rushes.', ['Find the drifting source and lock it', 'light', 'Weeks of animation saved.'], ['Deflicker in post', 'digi', 'It helps, and softens the image.']], '', 'Animation'],
    [3, 'Single-take staging and style studies', 'Advanced lighting workshops ask you to light the same set in different period styles (hard noir shadows, bright 1950s musical colour) and to cover a scene in one moving take.', 'For noir: one hard source, deep shadows, light through blinds. For the musical: high key, saturated colour, everyone visible. For the one-take: choreograph the camera and actors together, lighting for the whole path.', ['The one-take has a dead spot halfway.', ['Re-block so something happens there', 'move', 'The shot breathes all the way through.'], ['Hide a cut there', 'digi', 'It works, and the exercise was to not need it.']], '{P} lit {F} ({Y}) with noir rules and colour film; schools use the result as a style study.'],
  ],
  edt: [
    [1, 'Finding a story in trims', 'A school workshop gives editors 30 minutes of leftover picture and sound from old films and asks for a three-minute "personal statement". It teaches you to find meaning in material that was never meant to fit together.', 'Watch everything once without stopping. Log images by feeling, not content. Build around one image that moves you, and let sound make the bridges between unrelated shots.', ['Nothing in the material connects.', ['Let a sound carry across the cuts', 'sound', 'The audience reads it as one story.'], ['Add titles to explain it', 'struc', 'Now it\'s a slideshow.']], ''],
    [1, 'Editing documentary is writing', 'Without a script, the documentary editor writes the film in the cutting room: finding scenes, choosing structure, deciding whether narration is needed. It\'s trial and error, "working without a net".', 'Log everything. Build scenes first, without worrying about order. Then card them on a wall and test structures. Narration is the last resort, not the first.', ['The story only works with narration.', ['Find the scene that shows it instead', 'struc', 'The film trusts its audience.'], ['Write a short narration', 'dial', 'It works, and it sounds like television.']], '{E} found the story of {F} ({Y}) in the edit: the first assembly was four hours with no ending.', 'Documentary'],
    [1, 'Thirty seconds of story', 'Editing commercials teaches compression: setup, turn and payoff in 30 seconds, while staying on the brand\'s message.', 'Find the single image that sells it, then work backwards. Cut on movement to save frames. Check: could someone describe the ad after one viewing?', ['The client wants three more product shots.', ['Give them one hero shot that does the job', 'pack', 'The ad stays an ad people remember.'], ['Fit them all in', 'speed', 'It\'s a catalogue now.']], ''],
    [2, 'The assistant editor\'s job', 'Assistant editors keep the cutting room running: ingesting and syncing dailies, organising bins, managing turnovers to sound, music and VFX, and solving technical problems before the editor sees them.', 'Organise bins by scene, name everything the same way, keep a turnover log, and test every export before it leaves the room.', ['A turnover to sound has missing media.', ['Trace and fix it before anyone asks', 'cont', 'Sound never knows there was a problem.'], ['Send it and fix it later', 'speed', 'Sound loses a day.']], ''],
    [2, 'Cutting multicamera drama', 'Episodic series often shoot with several cameras. Editors learn to group angles, cut live-style between them, and still shape performance and pace like a single-camera drama.', 'Group clips by timecode, watch all angles of each take, then cut the performance first and the coverage second.', ['The best performance is on the worst angle.', ['Use it and hide the flaw with a cutaway', 'shape', 'The performance carries it.'], ['Use the better angle\'s weaker take', 'rhythm', 'Pretty and flat.']], ''],
    [3, 'The politics of the cutting room', 'Editors sit between director, producers and studio. Courses teach the "soft skills": managing decisions under pressure, protecting the director\'s cut, and keeping trust when notes conflict.', 'Listen to every note, try the important ones, and show rather than argue. When notes conflict, put both versions side by side and let the film decide.', ['The studio and director want opposite endings.', ['Cut both and screen them', 'cha', 'The film picks, and nobody loses face.'], ['Side with the director', 'eth', 'Loyal, and the studio stops calling you.']], ''],
    [3, 'Temp music and the music editor', 'Editors cut with temp music, and music editors later fit the score: stems, cue lengths, and edits that make a cue land on the cut. Courses give students a composer\'s stems to re-cut for new scenes.', 'Cut music on the beat or phrase, not the bar line. Use the score\'s stems to remove instruments where dialogue needs space.', ['The temp track is so good everyone loves it.', ['Warn the director early: it can\'t stay', 'score', 'The composer gets a fair chance.'], ['Keep cutting to it', 'rhythm', 'Temp love. The real score never wins.']], ''],
  ],
  snd: [
    [1, 'Room tone and wild tracks', 'Production sound mixers record room tone (silence in the location with the crew still) and wild tracks (lines or effects without the camera) so the dialogue editor can fill gaps and fix lines later.', 'After the last take in each location, call for thirty seconds of room tone with everyone frozen. Grab wild lines when a take had noise on a key word.', ['The crew groan when you ask for room tone at wrap.', ['Ask anyway, politely and quickly', 'sound', 'Post thanks you for months.'], ['Skip it', 'speed', 'The dialogue editor builds fake tone from scraps.']], '']
  ],
  mus: [
    [1, 'Rescoring a scene', 'Composing courses set clips from existing films and series to rescore in different genres, then discuss them as a group: what did the music change about the scene?', 'Watch the clip with no music. Decide what the scene needs: tension, warmth, irony, nothing? Write a cue, then a second one that takes the opposite approach.', ['Your cue says exactly what the picture already says.', ['Score what the character hides instead', 'score', 'The scene gets a second layer.'], ['Make it bigger', 'orch', 'Louder, and still repeating the picture.']], ''],
    [1, 'Spotting and the temp track', 'In a spotting session, composer and director decide where music starts and stops and what each cue does. Directors often fall in love with temp music, and the composer has to understand what they love about it.', 'Go through the film with the director. For each cue: start, stop, purpose. When a temp track is involved, ask what it does for the scene, not which notes they like.', ['The director wants the temp track copied.', ['Find what it does and do that differently', 'score', 'They get the feeling and an original score.'], ['Write something close to it', 'speed', 'They\'re happy. So is the temp composer\'s lawyer.']], ''],
    [1, 'Writing for the instruments', 'Score study and orchestration teach how instruments really behave: ranges, what\'s easy and hard to play, how combinations blend. Samples can play anything, and players can\'t.', 'Study a score while listening. Write a short cue for a small ensemble, check every part for playability, and hear it played by real musicians if you can.', ['A sampled line sounds great, and the players can\'t perform it.', ['Rewrite it for what the instrument does well', 'orch', 'It sounds better live than the samples.'], ['Keep it and hope', 'score', 'The session runs over budget while they practise.']], ''],
    [2, 'The recording session', 'Composers record with session musicians in studios: prepared parts, a click track, a conductor or the composer in the booth, and a clock that costs money every minute.', 'Prepare clean parts, a session plan in cue order, and the click tracks. Record the hardest cues first while players are fresh. Know which takes you\'ll keep.', ['You\'re running out of studio time.', ['Drop a minor cue and finish the big ones', 'tas', 'The film gets its key music live.'], ['Rush every cue', 'speed', 'Everything recorded, nothing quite right.']], ''],
    [2, 'Stems, mixing and delivery', 'Composers deliver mixed stems (strings, brass, percussion, synths and so on) so the dubbing mixer can balance music against dialogue. Wrong delivery formats can cost a day in the mix.', 'Agree the stem list and format with the mixer early. Name files consistently. Check every stem sums back to your full mix.', ['The mixer needs the strings without the percussion.', ['Send the stems you prepared', 'score', 'Fixed in minutes.'], ['Remix the cue from scratch', 'speed', 'A day lost, and the cue is different.']], ''],
    [2, 'Music for games', 'Game music must react to the player: loops that can repeat without tiring, layers that add as tension rises, and stingers for events. Composers work with game designers to implement it.', 'Write in layers that work alone and together. Plan transitions. Test it while playing, and listen for the moment the loop gets annoying.', ['Players turn your music off after an hour.', ['Add variations and silence between loops', 'score', 'Players leave it on.'], ['Make the loop longer', 'speed', 'It takes longer to annoy them.']], ''],
    [3, 'The language of the masters', 'Composition courses study the composers who shaped film music\'s language, and post-romantic techniques such as polytonality, parallel chords and harmonies built from intervals.', 'Analyse a famous cue for its harmonic trick. Write a short cue using one technique, such as parallel triads, to make an ordinary scene feel strange.', ['Your homage sounds too much like its model.', ['Keep the technique and change everything else', 'score', 'Influence, not imitation.'], ['Keep it; it\'s a tribute', 'orch', 'A tribute act.']], ''],
    [3, 'Royalties and publishing', 'A composer\'s income isn\'t just the fee: performance royalties when the film airs, publishing splits, and buyout deals that give up future income. The business sessions are as important as orchestration.', 'Read the contract for: who owns the publishing, whether it\'s a "package" deal (you pay for musicians out of your fee), and how cue sheets are filed so royalties reach you.', ['A studio offers a big fee with a full buyout.', ['Negotiate to keep a share of the publishing', 'fin', 'Less now, an income for decades.'], ['Take the fee', 'mkt', 'Good money, and nothing when it airs forever.']], ''],
  ],
  des: [
    [1, 'The measured survey', 'Production design courses start with a survey: measuring a real building, then drawing plans and elevations to scale. It teaches technical drawing and how architecture works.', 'Measure every wall, door and window with the heights. Sketch on site, then draw a clean plan and the elevations at 1:50, with the measurements dimensioned.', ['Your plan doesn\'t close: the walls don\'t meet.', ['Go back and re-measure', 'sets', 'It closes, and you learn to triangulate.'], ['Fudge the last wall', 'speed', 'The set built from it won\'t fit either.']], ''],
    [1, 'Set sketching in perspective', 'Designers must sketch environments quickly and convincingly: one- and two-point perspective, light and shadow for volume, and rendering materials so people believe them.', 'Set the horizon at the camera height. Find the vanishing points from the plan. Block in the volumes, then add light, shadow and texture.', ['Your sketch looks right and feels wrong.', ['Check the camera height and the lens', 'eye', 'Now it looks like a frame from the film.'], ['Add more detail', 'sets', 'More detail, same wrong feeling.']], ''],
    [1, 'Character of place', 'A design exercise: build a still image that shows the character of a place using the fewest elements, where the objects you choose and where you put them tell the story, with light setting the mood.', 'Choose three objects that tell you who lives here and what just happened. Place them for the camera. Light it with one idea.', ['Your set is full of nice props.', ['Take away everything that doesn\'t tell the story', 'sets', 'What\'s left tells it clearly.'], ['Keep them; it looks lived-in', 'world', 'It looks like a prop store.']], ''],
    [2, 'Set decoration and the buyer', 'Set decorators and buyers research the period, visit prop houses, and choose and dress every object. Decoration is how a set becomes a home, an office or a crime scene.', 'Research the period and the character\'s money and taste. Make a shopping list per set. Photograph options at the prop house and choose with the designer.', ['The perfect sofa is over budget.', ['Find a cheaper one and reupholster it', 'bud', 'Right look, right price.'], ['Buy it and cut elsewhere', 'sets', 'Great sofa. A bare kitchen.']], ''],
    [2, 'Sets with a set extension', 'Many sets are built only as high and wide as the camera needs, with VFX extending them. Designers draw where the build stops and the digital set begins, and the two must match.', 'Decide the build line with the DP and VFX. Build that line solidly, with real textures to sample. Give VFX the drawings and photographs.', ['The build line is in the middle of a key shot.', ['Move it to an architectural break', 'sets', 'The join is invisible.'], ['Leave it; VFX will cope', 'digi', 'They cope, at a cost.']], ''],
    [2, 'The white model', 'Before a set is built, designers make a white card model (and, more and more, a 3D digital model) for the director, DP and construction to walk around in miniature.', 'Build the model at scale, with removable walls where the camera goes. Use a lens viewfinder or a 3D camera to test shots with the director.', ['The director wants a wall moved after construction starts.', ['Show the cost in the model and offer options', 'tas', 'You find a version that works for both.'], ['Agree and tell construction', 'cha', 'The director is happy. The budget isn\'t.']], ''],
    [3, 'Presenting a design', 'Designers win jobs in interviews by presenting research and ideas: images that show atmosphere and environment, and a clear view of what the film is about.', 'Bring ten images, not a hundred. Explain each in one sentence linked to the story. Know the budget implications of your idea before you\'re asked.', ['The director asks how you\'d do it for less.', ['Have the cheaper version ready', 'bud', 'You get the job.'], ['Explain why the full version is needed', 'pres', 'You\'re right, and they hire someone cheaper.']], ''],
  ],
  cos: [
    [1, 'Cutting, draping and tailoring', 'Costume courses build the foundation skills: pattern cutting, draping, tailoring, alteration and finishing, and knowing fabrics, anatomy and how clothes read on camera.', 'Draft a basic block, toile it in calico, fit it on a body, correct the pattern, and only then cut the real fabric.', ['You\'re tempted to skip the toile.', ['Make the toile', 'wardrobe', 'The fitting is quick and the garment fits.'], ['Cut straight into the fabric', 'speed', 'Expensive fabric, a costly mistake.']], ''],
    [1, 'From painting to costume', 'In a cross-department workshop, costume designers translate an historical painting into screen-ready costumes, working with the set, light and camera. Paint and cloth behave differently on camera.', 'Study the painting\'s silhouettes, colours and textures. Find fabrics that read the same under film light. Test them on camera before you commit.', ['A colour in the painting turns muddy on camera.', ['Test fabrics under the DP\'s light', 'wardrobe', 'You find the colour that reads.'], ['Match the painting exactly', 'period', 'Accurate, and it looks wrong.']], ''],
    [2, 'Specialist making', 'Specialist workshops cover period practice, leatherwork, moulding and fabrication, futuristic materials, corsetry, millinery and breakdown (ageing and distressing clothes so they look lived in).', 'Breakdown: sand, wash, dye and wear the garment where the body would wear it: elbows, knees, cuffs, collars. Make several copies at different levels of wear for continuity and stunts.', ['A hero coat is needed for a stunt and a close-up.', ['Make matching copies at the same level of wear', 'cont', 'Stunt and close-up cut together.'], ['Use one coat and be careful', 'wardrobe', 'It tears in the stunt on day two.']], ''],
    [2, 'Script breakdown, fittings and continuity', 'On a production, the costume designer breaks the script down into story days and changes per character, runs fittings, budgets the department and keeps the continuity book.', 'List each character\'s changes per story day. Plan fittings early with photos. Keep a continuity photo for every scene, including how the clothes are worn.', ['The schedule shoots the end of the film first.', ['Plan the wear and ageing backwards from the end', 'cont', 'The costume ages correctly when cut in order.'], ['Deal with it as you go', 'speed', 'The dress is clean in a later scene.']], ''],
    [3, 'The garment commission', 'A long project: a fully realised garment from a production brief, treated like a professional commission with research, pattern, toile, fittings, construction, finishing, budget and documentation.', 'Write the brief back to yourself in your own words: character, scene, action, camera. Keep a record of every decision and its cost.', ['The brief changes halfway through.', ['Re-plan the budget and timeline and tell the client', 'bud', 'You deliver on time, as a professional would.'], ['Absorb it quietly', 'wardrobe', 'You deliver late and exhausted.']], ''],
    [3, 'Head of department', 'Leading costume on a production means more than design: managing a team, a budget and a schedule, and balancing ambition against money and time.', 'Delegate making to your team and keep design and fittings for yourself. Review the budget every week. Say early what you can\'t do.', ['A crowd scene needs fifty more costumes than budgeted.', ['Hire from a costume house and adapt', 'bud', 'Fifty people dressed and on budget.'], ['Make them all', 'wardrobe', 'Beautiful, late and over budget.']], ''],
  ],
  pro: [
    [1, 'Breaking down a script', 'Producing courses start with the breakdown: marking every element (cast, extras, locations, props, vehicles, effects) on every scene, then building a schedule from it in scheduling software.', 'Colour-code each element. Make a strip for each scene with its page count in eighths. Group strips by location, then cast availability, then day and night.', ['The schedule has the lead working one day out of every three.', ['Group their scenes to cut their days', 'bud', 'You save a week of star money.'], ['Shoot in story order', 'setm', 'Simpler for the actors and much more expensive.']], ''],
    [1, 'From schedule to budget', 'The budget is built from the schedule: every shooting day costs crew, kit and locations, with above-the-line costs (talent, rights) and below-the-line costs (everything else) plus post, delivery and contingency.', 'Price each department from the schedule. Add post-production and delivery, then insurance and contingency, usually about ten percent.', ['The budget is 20% over.', ['Find the schedule changes that save days', 'bud', 'Fewer days is the biggest saving.'], ['Cut the contingency', 'fin', 'On paper it balances. In reality it\'s a gamble.']], ''],
    [1, 'Coverage and the weekend read', 'Producers read constantly and write coverage: a summary, a comment, and a verdict (pass, consider, recommend). The skill is saying clearly and strategically what works and what doesn\'t.', 'Read the script in one sitting. Write a one-paragraph logline and summary, then a page of comments on premise, character, structure and market. End with a verdict.', ['A script is brilliant and unsellable.', ['Say both, clearly', 'mkt', 'Your boss trusts your reports.'], ['Pass on it', 'tas', 'Safe, and you might miss a hit.']], ''],
    [2, 'The finance waterfall', 'Film finance is a waterfall: when money comes in, it flows to the distributors\' fees and costs first, then to recoup the financiers, then to deferments and profit participants. Producers must understand who gets paid when.', 'Draw the waterfall for your film: the distribution fee, P&A costs, the sales agent\'s commission, the equity with its premium, then the backend split.', ['An investor wants to be paid first.', ['Offer a premium instead of moving up the order', 'fin', 'The other financiers stay in.'], ['Give them first position', 'talent', 'One investor happy; the rest walk away.']], ''],
    [2, 'Pre-sales and gap', 'Independent films are often financed by selling distribution rights territory by territory before production (pre-sales), with a bank lending against those contracts, and "gap" finance covering what\'s left at higher risk.', 'With a sales agent, estimate each territory\'s value. Pre-sell the strongest markets. Borrow against the contracts, and keep the unsold territories for later upside.', ['Pre-selling everything would fully finance the film.', ['Keep some territories back', 'fin', 'Less safe, and room for the film to pay off.'], ['Sell everything', 'dist', 'It gets made, and any profits go to others.']], ''],
    [2, 'Developing IP for television', 'Producers source intellectual property (books, true stories, podcasts), option it, and partner with writers to develop a proposal for a series, then pitch it to commissioners.', 'Option the rights with a time limit and a purchase price. Pair it with a writer whose voice suits it. Develop a pitch document and an episode outline.', ['The author wants approval over the script.', ['Offer consultation instead of approval', 'talent', 'The author feels heard and the writer stays free.'], ['Give approval to close the deal', 'pack', 'The deal closes and development stalls.']], ''],
    [3, 'Union rules, incentives and locations', 'Prepping a feature means choosing a location for its tax incentives, working with union agreements, setting up a single-purpose company, running payroll and reading cost reports.', 'Compare incentives by what you\'ll really spend locally. Budget union minimums and overtime rules. Read the weekly cost report and act on the variances.', ['A state offers a big rebate, but no crew live there.', ['Price in travel and lodging before choosing', 'bud', 'You choose the place that is really cheapest.'], ['Take the rebate', 'fin', 'The rebate is spent on hotel rooms.']], ''],
    [3, 'Is it worth it? Five questions', 'A producing protocol asks of every project: Is it an emotional experience? Will talent want it? What\'s it worth? Can it be made? Will it sell? There is really only one question: is it worth years of your life?', 'Score a project honestly on each question. Notice where you\'re making excuses. Pass on projects you only like.', ['A project scores high everywhere but you don\'t love it.', ['Pass and keep looking', 'tas', 'You spend your years on the right film.'], ['Make it; it\'s commercial', 'mkt', 'It makes money and you can\'t remember it.']], ''],
    [3, 'Clearances and delivery', 'A film isn\'t finished until it\'s delivered: licences for music and footage, clearances for logos and artworks, E&O insurance, and a long list of technical and legal deliverables.', 'Keep a clearance log from day one. License music with the right media and territory. Check every logo and artwork in frame. Deliver to the distributor\'s spec sheet.', ['A song in the film isn\'t cleared for TV.', ['Pay for the extra licence now', 'fin', 'The TV sale goes through.'], ['Replace it for TV only', 'speed', 'The TV version loses its best scene.']], ''],
    [2, 'Cost reports', 'Every week the production manager reports spending against budget: actual costs, commitments and the estimated final cost, by department. The variances show where money is going wrong.', 'Get the accountant\'s figures every week. For each overspend, ask why and whether it will continue. Move money from savings to overspends before it\'s too late.', ['Catering is overspending every week.', ['Find the cause: more crew than budgeted', 'bud', 'You fix the real problem.'], ['Cut the catering', 'cha', 'The crew\'s mood drops with the food.']], ''],
    [2, 'The post-production producer', 'Post producers run the path from wrap to delivery: schedule, budget, vendors, people. Post works differently on a studio film, an indie, a network series and a streaming series.', 'Make a post schedule backwards from delivery: the mix, the grade, VFX, picture lock, the cut. Book facilities early. Track every vendor\'s deadline.', ['Picture lock slips by two weeks.', ['Re-plan every vendor at once', 'tas', 'Delivery holds.'], ['Hope to catch it up', 'speed', 'Delivery slips.']], ''],
  ],
  dist: [
    [2, 'Windows and platforms', 'A film\'s release is planned in windows: cinemas, then premium rental, then streaming or pay TV, then free TV. Each window\'s length is negotiated, and each one sells the next.', 'Map the windows for your film and price each. Ask which window is most valuable and protect it.', ['A streamer offers to skip cinemas entirely.', ['Weigh the guaranteed fee against the theatrical upside', 'dist', 'You choose with the numbers in front of you.'], ['Take the money', 'fin', 'Safe; the film is gone in a weekend.']], ''],
    [2, 'Positioning a film', 'Marketing starts with positioning: who is this for, what promise does it make, and what makes it different from what\'s already out? Demographics, PR, social media and creative advertising all follow.', 'Write one sentence: "For [audience] who [want], this is the film that [promise]." Every poster, trailer and post must deliver that sentence.', ['The film has two audiences who want different things.', ['Run two campaigns aimed at each', 'mkt', 'Both find it.'], ['Average the message', 'pack', 'Neither finds it.']], ''],
    [3, 'Markets and festivals', 'Films are bought and sold at markets and festivals, where sales agents screen to buyers from every territory. Producing students work at major markets to see buying and selling up close.', 'Book screenings early. Bring a one-sheet and a trailer. Know which buyers matter in each territory and what they paid last year.', ['A buyer makes an early offer before the premiere.', ['Hold until after the screening if the buzz is good', 'dist', 'The price doubles.'], ['Take it', 'fin', 'Safe, if the screening goes badly.']], ''],
  ],
  doc: [
    [1, 'Observation', 'Documentary courses start with observation: capturing meaning in a situation without interference or manipulation. Patient, well-managed observation shows character and story.', 'Spend time with your subject before you film. Shoot long takes and let things happen. Never ask anyone to "do that again".', ['Nothing is happening.', ['Keep watching', 'eye', 'Then something does.'], ['Ask them to do something interesting', 'eth', 'You get a scene and lose the truth.']], '', 'Documentary'],
    [1, 'Casting a documentary', 'A film about change needs a person who embodies it, filmed in a short time. Finding the right character, and winning their trust, is most of the work.', 'Research widely and meet many people. Look for someone in the middle of a change with an outcome that\'s uncertain. Be honest with them about what the film is.', ['Your subject is charming and nothing will change for them.', ['Keep looking for someone in flux', 'eye', 'The new subject gives you a story.'], ['Film them anyway; they\'re great on camera', 'cha', 'A lovely portrait with no story.']], '', 'Documentary'],
    [1, 'The poetic short', 'Directors and sound designers are asked to set sound, image and words against each other in surprising but convincing ways, expressing an idea or feeling very economically.', 'Record sound separately from image. Pair them unexpectedly: a child\'s laughter over an empty factory. Cut until nothing is left to explain.', ['The juxtaposition is confusing, not surprising.', ['Find one clearer link between the two', 'sound', 'Strange, and clear.'], ['Add a caption to explain', 'struc', 'It\'s clear and no longer poetry.']], ''],
    [2, 'The question film', 'Directors pick a question about the world and make a film that tries to answer it. The heart of it is finding a subject ripe for questioning and being inventive and persistent, on schedule and on budget.', 'Phrase the question so it can\'t be answered with yes or no. List who might answer it and who would never say. Build a schedule and budget with a production manager.', ['The answer turns out to be boring.', ['Follow the better question it raised', 'eye', 'The film becomes a real enquiry.'], ['Push to the original answer', 'struc', 'A neat film about nothing much.']], '', 'Documentary'],
    [2, 'Interviews and archive', 'Interviewing is a craft: questions that invite stories, silence that gets the real answer. Archive footage needs research, licensing and an ethical eye for context.', 'Ask "tell me about the day..." instead of "how did you feel?". Wait after an answer. For archive, log the source and licence for every clip.', ['A key archive clip has no clear owner.', ['Keep researching the rights', 'eth', 'You find the owner and license it.'], ['Use it and hope', 'speed', 'The broadcaster won\'t take the film.']], ''],
    [3, 'The long-form pitch', 'Graduating documentary makers develop a feature or series idea as their calling card: a strong subject, unique access, and a reason it must be made now.', 'Write the logline, explain your access, show a short teaser of footage, and name the buyers it suits.', ['Your access is promised, not confirmed.', ['Get it in writing before you pitch', 'eth', 'Buyers trust you.'], ['Pitch it as confirmed', 'pres', 'You get the money, then have to deliver it.']], ''],
  ],
  anim: [
    [1, 'Animating with sand', 'Courses throw animators into unusual media like sand on a lightbox, where each frame is made by moving sand. The fluid medium teaches storytelling through morphing and transition.', 'Plan the transformations: every image must become the next. Shoot on twos, and let the music guide the timing.', ['Your story needs a cut, and sand flows.', ['Turn the cut into a transformation', 'digi', 'The medium becomes the meaning.'], ['Cut to black between shots', 'struc', 'It works, and loses the magic.']], '', 'Animation'],
    [1, 'Mission statement to animatic', 'Animation ideas start with a mission statement (what the film is about for you), then storyboards, then an animatic: the boards cut to time with a scratch soundtrack. Films are greenlit on the animatic.', 'Write the mission statement in one sentence. Board every shot. Time the animatic to a scratch track and watch it with fresh eyes the next day.', ['The animatic is too long.', ['Cut it now, before anything is animated', 'struc', 'Every cut saves weeks of animation.'], ['Animate and trim later', 'speed', 'You animate shots you\'ll throw away.']], '', 'Animation'],
    [2, 'The character film', 'Character design starts with personality traits, so the character can work in many stories. Animators pair characters, record actors for the dialogue, and animate gesture and lip-sync.', 'Write five traits. Design a silhouette that shows them. Record the actors first and animate to their performance, acting it out yourself on video for reference.', ['The lip-sync is perfect and the character feels dead.', ['Animate the thought before the line', 'digi', 'The character thinks, then speaks.'], ['Add more mouth shapes', 'speed', 'More accurate, still dead.']], '', 'Animation'],
    [2, 'Pixilation', 'Pixilation is stop-motion with real people and objects, shot frame by frame, so people slide, float and appear. It teaches timing and how a place\'s character can drive a story.', 'Lock the camera. Move your actor a few centimetres per frame. Shoot at the same time of day so the light doesn\'t jump.', ['The light changes as clouds pass.', ['Wait for steady light, or shoot under cover', 'light', 'It flows.'], ['Keep shooting', 'speed', 'It flickers.']], ''],
    [3, 'Production reports and the pipeline', 'Animated films are tracked with fortnightly reports: shots finished against shots planned, budget, problems. A pipeline test before production finds technical problems early.', 'Put one shot through the whole pipeline before production starts. Track seconds of finished animation per week. Report problems the week they appear.', ['You\'re a week behind halfway through.', ['Re-plan and simplify shots now', 'tas', 'You finish on time.'], ['Work weekends', 'speed', 'You finish, exhausted, and a week late anyway.']], '', 'Animation'],
  ],
  ad: [
    [1, 'Trainee days', 'Assistant director trainee schemes require hundreds of days on set (one well-known scheme asks for 350 days over two years) on features, television and commercials, with written evaluations from the ADs and production managers after each job.', 'Be first in and last out. Learn every department head\'s name on day one. Ask for feedback at the end of each job, and act on it on the next one.', ['Your evaluation says "needs to anticipate more".', ['Shadow the 2nd AD and learn their checklist', 'setm', 'Next evaluation: "always one step ahead".'], ['Work harder', 'speed', 'You run faster in the wrong direction.']], ''],
    [1, 'Not a director in the making', 'Assistant directing is a career in itself. It usually leads to production management and producing rather than directing. The 1st is the director\'s right hand, the 2nd is the 1st\'s, and the trainee learns by doing.', 'Learn the chain: the 1st runs the floor, the 2nd runs the call sheet and the actors, the 2nd 2nd and trainees run the background and the base.', ['A director asks your opinion on a shot.', ['Give it briefly, then get back to the schedule', 'setm', 'Trusted for both.'], ['Pitch a whole new approach', 'vstory', 'The 1st AD has words with you.']], ''],
    [2, 'The call sheet and the production report', 'The 2nd AD writes the next day\'s call sheet (who, where, when, which scenes) and the daily production report records what really happened: times, scenes completed, delays and why.', 'Write the call sheet from the schedule, then check it against every department. In the report, be factual: "lost 40 minutes, generator fault".', ['The producer wants the delay left out of the report.', ['Report it accurately', 'eth', 'The insurer pays the claim later.'], ['Leave it out', 'cha', 'The producer is happy, until the claim.']], ''],
    [3, 'Running the floor', 'The 1st AD runs the set: rehearsal, lighting, shooting, moving on. They keep it calm and safe and manage the director\'s time, and they need to read the room.', 'Call each step clearly: "rehearsal", "final checks", "turning over". Warn departments before the next setup. Give the director a time check before it becomes a crisis.', ['The director wants one more take, and you\'re losing the light.', ['Give the honest time and let them choose', 'setm', 'They choose, and you make the day.'], ['Say no', 'tas', 'You make the day and lose the director.']], ''],
    [2, 'Risk assessments', 'Production managers write risk assessments for every location and stunt: hazards, who might be harmed, the controls, and who is responsible. Schools train every department in it.', 'Walk the location. List the hazards (traffic, water, height, weather, electrics). For each, write the control and name the person responsible. Brief everyone on the day.', ['The director wants a scene on a cliff edge.', ['Assess it and add a safety line and a spotter', 'setm', 'The shot is made safely.'], ['Trust the crew to be careful', 'speed', 'Fine, until it isn\'t.']], ''],
  ],
  vfx: [
    [1, 'Set extensions', 'VFX artists extend physical sets digitally, matching perspective, light and texture so the join is invisible. The art department draws where the build stops.', 'Get the set drawings and survey data. Photograph textures on set. Track the camera, build the extension, and match the grain and lens.', ['The real set and the extension don\'t match in colour.', ['Sample the plate and match it', 'digi', 'Seamless.'], ['Grade the whole shot to hide it', 'col', 'It looks graded.']], ''],
    [2, 'Real-time engines and virtual production', 'Game engines now render sets in real time for previs, virtual scouting and LED volumes. Designers, VFX and games artists share tools.', 'Build the set in the engine to scale. Scout it in VR with the director and DP. Lock the camera positions before the LED shoot.', ['The engine set looks great but the scale is wrong.', ['Check it against the plan in metres', 'digi', 'The actors fit the doors.'], ['Fix it on the day', 'speed', 'A day lost on an expensive stage.']], '', 'Sci-fi'],
    [2, 'Node-based compositing', 'Compositing is done in node-based software, where each operation (key, track, grade, merge) is a node in a tree. Clear trees are easier to fix when notes come.', 'Build the comp left to right: plates in, keys, cleanup, CG merge, grade, grain, out. Label every node. Keep a backdrop for each section.', ['A note comes in after you\'ve finished.', ['Change one node in your clean tree', 'digi', 'Five minutes.'], ['Rebuild the messy comp', 'speed', 'A day.']], ''],
  ],
  col: [
    [2, 'Colour spaces and LUTs', 'Modern colour work happens in managed colour spaces: camera log footage converted through LUTs or colour transforms to display spaces for cinema, HDR or streaming.', 'Agree the pipeline with the DP before the shoot: camera colour space, the on-set LUT, the working space for the grade, and the output formats.', ['The on-set LUT doesn\'t match the grade.', ['Build the grade from the same transforms', 'col', 'What the DP saw on set is where you start.'], ['Ignore it and start fresh', 'colour', 'The DP is lost in the grade.']], ''],
    [3, 'Grading a graduation film', 'Colourists grade the final films with the director and DP: shot matching, then the look, then secondaries and windows, in the time available.', 'Balance and match every scene first. Then set the look with the DP. Spend the last time on the key scenes and hero shots.', ['There\'s one day left and two reels to grade.', ['Match everything, then style the key scenes', 'col', 'A consistent film with great moments.'], ['Perfect reel one', 'colour', 'Reel one is beautiful. The rest is ungraded.']], ''],
  ],
  post: [
    [1, 'Deconstructing the soundtrack', 'Sound courses begin by taking soundtracks apart: atmospheres, effects, Foley, dialogue and music, each one a layer that contributes to the overall effect.', 'Mute a scene\'s layers one at a time and note what each one does. Then rebuild it from silence, adding atmos first, then Foley, then effects, then dialogue.', ['The scene feels empty with only dialogue.', ['Build the atmosphere underneath', 'sdes', 'The room becomes a place.'], ['Add music', 'score', 'It covers the problem and hides the place.']], ''],
    [1, 'Picture to sound', 'In a classic exercise the sound designers make a soundtrack first, and animators make a film to it. It reverses the usual order and shows how much sound can drive a story.', 'Design a soundtrack with a clear shape: a world, an event, a change. Make it work as a story with your eyes shut.', ['Your soundtrack is beautiful but static.', ['Add an event that changes everything', 'sdes', 'The animator has a story to tell.'], ['Make it richer', 'sound', 'Richer and still static.']], ''],
    [2, 'The tracklay and the mix', 'Post sound runs on schedules like "three weeks tracklay, five days mix". Tracklaying is building every layer; the mix is the balancing, placing and shaping in a dubbing theatre.', 'In the tracklay, lay alternatives so the mixer has choices. Split dialogue by character and mic. Label everything. In the mix, decide the one thing the audience must hear in each moment.', ['Day four of five in the mix, and you\'re behind.', ['Prioritise the key scenes and simplify the rest', 'tas', 'You finish, and the key scenes sing.'], ['Keep detailing reel one', 'sound', 'Reel one is perfect. Reel four is a rush.']], ''],
    [2, 'Mixing a film you didn\'t edit', 'Schools copy the industry by having a colleague mix your film. The mixer\'s fresh ears hear what the editor is too close to notice, and the director gets new creative ideas.', 'As the editor, hand over clean, labelled sessions and a note of your intentions. As the mixer, watch the film once with no notes before you touch a fader.', ['The mixer drops a sound effect you love.', ['Ask what they heard without it', 'cha', 'The scene is quieter, and better.'], ['Insist it goes back in', 'sound', 'It goes back. The mixer was right.']], ''],
    [2, 'ADR and dialogue repair', 'ADR (re-recording dialogue in a studio) is the last resort after noise reduction and alternate takes. Sound editors prep the cue list, run the session for sync and performance, and edit the result to match production sound.', 'Search for alternate takes first. Then try noise reduction gently. For ADR, play the original line three times in the actor\'s headphones, and record in the same mic perspective.', ['An actor\'s ADR is perfect in sync and dead in feeling.', ['Have them move as they did in the scene', 'sound', 'The breath and energy come back.'], ['Use it; sync is what matters', 'speed', 'The audience feels the join.']], ''],
    [3, 'Creature voices and synthesis', 'Creatures, monsters and machines are built from layers: animal recordings, human vocalisations, synthesis, pitched and processed until the source is unrecognisable.', 'Record a range of real sources. Layer a body (low), a voice (middle) and a texture (high). Make each emotion its own sound: threat, pain, curiosity.', ['The creature sounds scary but not alive.', ['Give it breath and small sounds between roars', 'sdes', 'It feels like an animal, not a sound effect.'], ['Make the roar bigger', 'sound', 'Bigger, and still a sound effect.']], 'The creature in {F} ({Y}) is three animals and the sound designer\'s own breathing, pitched down.', 'Horror'],
    [3, 'Mixing in surround and object audio', 'Big mixes are done in 5.1, 7.1 or object-based formats where sounds can be placed overhead and moved around the room. More speakers mean more choices, and more ways to distract.', 'Keep dialogue in the centre. Use surrounds for the world and for the moments when the audience should feel surrounded. Save overhead movement for when the story needs it.', ['Every sound is flying around the room.', ['Pull back and save movement for the big moment', 'sound', 'When it moves, the audience ducks.'], ['Leave it; it\'s exciting', 'sdes', 'Exciting for five minutes, then tiring.']], '', 'Action'],
    [2, 'The deliverables list', 'Distributors deliver to a spec: picture masters, audio stems, subtitles, closed captions, M&E tracks (music and effects without dialogue, for dubbing), artwork and paperwork.', 'Get the delivery schedule early. Plan the M&E in the mix. Build a checklist and tick every item with the file name and date.', ['There\'s no M&E track and the film has sold abroad.', ['Rebuild it from the stems', 'sound', 'Costly, and it saves the sale.'], ['Ask the buyer to accept subtitles only', 'dist', 'They reduce the price.']], ''],
  ],
  stage: [
    [2, 'New writing and the dramaturg', 'New plays are developed with a dramaturg, then rehearsed with a director and actors, and performed for audiences in new-writing venues that film and TV people watch closely for new voices.', 'In rehearsal, listen to the actors\' questions; they show you what\'s unclear. Cut what the staging makes unnecessary. Keep the play\'s one big question alive.', ['An actor wants a line changed every day.', ['Listen for the pattern behind the requests', 'dial', 'The scene finds its real shape.'], ['Refuse; the text is the text', 'struc', 'The actor plays it unconvinced.']], ''],
  ],
  pod: [
    [1, 'Storytelling without images', 'Writers, sound designers and composers make audio dramas together to explore storytelling in sound alone, where every location and action must be heard.', 'Write sound cues into the script. Build each space with atmosphere. Use perspective (close, distant) as your camera.', ['Listeners can\'t tell where the scene is.', ['Establish the place with sound in the first seconds', 'sdes', 'They\'re there before a word is said.'], ['Have a character say where they are', 'dial', '"Here we are in the kitchen."']], ''],
  ],
  creator: [
    [2, 'Short games and playtesting', 'Small teams build short, playable games, often five or ten minutes long, around one mechanic or one meaningful moment, and test them on real players early and often.', 'Find the one mechanic. Build the ugliest playable version in a day. Watch people play without helping. Fix what confuses them.', ['Players miss the point of your game.', ['Change the design, not the instructions', 'digi', 'They get it without being told.'], ['Add a tutorial screen', 'struc', 'They skip it.']], ''],
  ],
  mkp: [
    [1, 'Breakdown and continuity', 'Hair and make-up designers break the script down by character and story day: injuries, ageing, weather, sweat, tears. Continuity photos are taken before every take.', 'Make a chart per character: story day, look, and any changes (a cut on day 3, healing by day 6). Photograph the look from front, side and back.', ['A cut heals in the wrong order because of the schedule.', ['Plan the stages backwards from the chart', 'cont', 'It heals correctly on screen.'], ['Judge it on the day', 'mkup', 'The audience notices.']], ''],
    [2, 'Life casts and prosthetics', 'Prosthetics start with a life cast of the actor\'s face, then sculpting in clay, moulding, and running pieces in silicone or foam latex, then applying and painting them so they move with the face.', 'Cast the actor carefully: talk them through it, keep their airways clear. Sculpt thin at the edges. Test-apply under set light before the shoot.', ['The prosthetic edges show in close-up.', ['Run thinner edges and blend them better', 'mkup', 'Invisible.'], ['Ask the DP to light softer', 'cha', 'The DP sighs and does it.']], ''],
  ],
};
for (const f in CURR_DEEP) if (CURR[f]) CURR[f].lessons.push(...CURR_DEEP[f]);
